1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,873
A quarter turn of a bicycle
wheel is ninety degrees.

2
00:00:03,873 --> 00:00:06,533
A full turn is three hundred and sixty.

3
00:00:06,933 --> 00:00:09,389
But who chose three hundred and sixty?

4
00:00:09,389 --> 00:00:10,468
Not the wheel.

5
00:00:10,468 --> 00:00:11,368
People did.

6
00:00:11,798 --> 00:00:14,995
Aarav thought about this
while riding to college today.

7
00:00:15,345 --> 00:00:18,000
His wheel rolls along
the road as it turns.

8
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,565
Could the road tell us how far it turned?

9
00:00:21,065 --> 00:00:21,965
It can.

10
00:00:21,833 --> 00:00:26,835
That idea is called the radian, and
it opens a new chapter: Chapter 3,

11
00:00:26,835 --> 00:00:28,510
Trigonometric Functions.

12
00:00:29,160 --> 00:00:30,752
First, what is an angle?

13
00:00:31,102 --> 00:00:33,459
An angle is a measure of rotation.

14
00:00:33,459 --> 00:00:35,965
A ray turns about its starting point.

15
00:00:36,366 --> 00:00:38,710
The ray starts on the initial side.

16
00:00:38,710 --> 00:00:41,103
Where it stops is the terminal side.

17
00:00:41,103 --> 00:00:43,700
The point it turns about is the vertex.

18
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:45,124
Watch it turn.

19
00:00:45,124 --> 00:00:47,778
The more it rotates, the bigger the angle.

20
00:00:47,778 --> 00:00:49,844
Now we need a unit to count it.

21
00:00:50,494 --> 00:00:52,451
The first unit is the degree.

22
00:00:52,801 --> 00:00:57,137
Cut one full turn into three
hundred and sixty equal slices.

23
00:00:57,137 --> 00:00:59,077
One slice is one degree.

24
00:00:59,477 --> 00:01:04,019
For finer work, one degree is cut into
sixty minutes, and one minute into

25
00:01:04,019 --> 00:01:05,045
sixty seconds.

26
00:01:05,445 --> 00:01:08,707
Three hundred and sixty
splits evenly in many ways.

27
00:01:08,707 --> 00:01:11,277
But it is still a number people chose.

28
00:01:11,927 --> 00:01:12,827
Quick one.

29
00:01:12,771 --> 00:01:16,564
Can an angle be bigger than
three hundred and sixty degrees?

30
00:01:16,564 --> 00:01:17,757
Pause and think.

31
00:01:20,957 --> 00:01:21,857
Yes!

32
00:01:21,493 --> 00:01:23,384
The ray can keep turning.

33
00:01:23,384 --> 00:01:28,301
One full turn plus sixty degrees more
is four hundred and twenty degrees.

34
00:01:28,951 --> 00:01:30,295
Now the second unit.

35
00:01:30,295 --> 00:01:32,613
This one comes from the circle itself.

36
00:01:32,963 --> 00:01:35,560
Take a circle whose radius is one unit.

37
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:37,363
We call it a unit circle.

38
00:01:37,763 --> 00:01:41,027
Mark an arc on its rim
that is also one unit long.

39
00:01:41,427 --> 00:01:44,682
The angle this arc makes at
the centre is one radian.

40
00:01:45,332 --> 00:01:49,812
In any circle, when the arc is as
long as the radius, the angle is one

41
00:01:49,812 --> 00:01:50,712
radian.

42
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:51,740
Now watch.

43
00:01:51,630 --> 00:01:54,572
Lay the radius around
the rim, again and again.

44
00:01:54,572 --> 00:01:57,078
One, two, three, four, five, six.

45
00:01:57,478 --> 00:02:00,285
Six radii fit, and a small gap is left.

46
00:02:00,285 --> 00:02:03,901
All the way round is about
six point two eight radians.

47
00:02:03,901 --> 00:02:05,532
That number is two pi.

48
00:02:06,182 --> 00:02:09,032
So how do we find the angle for any arc?

49
00:02:09,032 --> 00:02:10,312
Four small steps.

50
00:02:10,662 --> 00:02:11,562
Step one.

51
00:02:11,553 --> 00:02:14,977
An arc as long as the
radius makes one radian.

52
00:02:15,727 --> 00:02:16,627
Step two.

53
00:02:16,603 --> 00:02:19,678
Equal arcs make equal
angles at the centre.

54
00:02:19,678 --> 00:02:22,817
So an arc two radii
long makes two radians.

55
00:02:23,567 --> 00:02:24,622
Step three.

56
00:02:24,622 --> 00:02:29,074
So the angle in radians is
how many radii fit in the arc.

57
00:02:29,074 --> 00:02:30,967
Theta equals l over r.

58
00:02:31,717 --> 00:02:32,721
Step four.

59
00:02:32,721 --> 00:02:34,797
Multiply both sides by r.

60
00:02:34,797 --> 00:02:37,830
The arc length l equals r times theta.

61
00:02:38,580 --> 00:02:40,438
Check it on a full turn.

62
00:02:40,438 --> 00:02:43,148
The arc is the whole rim, two pi r.

63
00:02:43,148 --> 00:02:45,694
Divide by r, and theta is two pi.

64
00:02:46,344 --> 00:02:47,430
Remember this.

65
00:02:47,430 --> 00:02:51,197
One radian is the angle where
the arc equals the radius.

66
00:02:51,897 --> 00:02:53,028
Side by side.

67
00:02:53,028 --> 00:02:57,072
Degrees cut a full turn into
three hundred and sixty parts.

68
00:02:57,072 --> 00:02:59,440
That number was chosen by people.

69
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:01,869
Radians count the arc in radii.

70
00:03:01,869 --> 00:03:05,118
A full turn is two pi,
about six point two eight.

71
00:03:05,118 --> 00:03:07,288
That number comes from the circle.

72
00:03:07,888 --> 00:03:12,304
A radian is a length divided by
a length, so it has no real unit.

73
00:03:12,304 --> 00:03:15,846
When an angle has no unit
written, it is in radians.

74
00:03:16,446 --> 00:03:17,477
Now you try.

75
00:03:17,477 --> 00:03:22,394
A tiny wheel and a giant wheel each
roll an arc equal to their own radius.

76
00:03:22,394 --> 00:03:25,118
Which one turns through the bigger angle?

77
00:03:25,118 --> 00:03:26,350
Pause and think.

78
00:03:29,550 --> 00:03:30,450
Neither!

79
00:03:30,297 --> 00:03:32,401
Both turn exactly one radian.

80
00:03:32,401 --> 00:03:35,767
The radian does not care
how big the circle is.

81
00:03:36,417 --> 00:03:38,152
Now, one worked example.

82
00:03:38,502 --> 00:03:41,898
Aarav's wheel has a radius
of thirty five centimetres.

83
00:03:41,898 --> 00:03:44,277
His bicycle rolls forward one metre.

84
00:03:44,277 --> 00:03:47,820
Through what angle, in
radians, does the wheel turn?

85
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:49,220
Step one.

86
00:03:49,125 --> 00:03:53,474
When a wheel rolls without slipping,
the road it covers equals the arc

87
00:03:53,474 --> 00:03:55,035
that touched the road.

88
00:03:55,035 --> 00:03:56,459
So l is one metre.

89
00:03:57,209 --> 00:03:58,126
Step two.

90
00:03:58,126 --> 00:03:59,737
Use the same units.

91
00:03:59,737 --> 00:04:02,488
One metre is one hundred centimetres.

92
00:04:03,238 --> 00:04:04,228
Step three.

93
00:04:04,228 --> 00:04:08,660
Theta equals l over r, which is
one hundred over thirty five.

94
00:04:09,410 --> 00:04:10,310
Step four.

95
00:04:10,281 --> 00:04:14,346
That is twenty over seven, about
two point eight six radians.

96
00:04:15,096 --> 00:04:19,148
That is less than half of two pi, so
the wheel turned a little less than

97
00:04:19,148 --> 00:04:20,048
half a turn.

98
00:04:20,684 --> 00:04:22,149
Now, the exam trap.

99
00:04:22,499 --> 00:04:26,125
Arc length for one full
turn, radius ten centimetres.

100
00:04:26,125 --> 00:04:30,283
Many students write l equals ten
times three hundred and sixty.

101
00:04:30,683 --> 00:04:34,017
But l equals r theta
needs theta in radians.

102
00:04:34,017 --> 00:04:39,335
A full turn is two pi, so l is ten
times two pi, about sixty two point

103
00:04:39,335 --> 00:04:40,751
eight centimetres.

104
00:04:41,401 --> 00:04:42,911
Where is this asked?

105
00:04:42,911 --> 00:04:48,140
In the PUC board exams: given an arc
and a radius, find the angle in radians,

106
00:04:48,140 --> 00:04:49,863
or the other way round.

107
00:04:50,463 --> 00:04:51,827
Exam level now.

108
00:04:51,827 --> 00:04:53,666
Find theta in radians.

109
00:04:53,666 --> 00:04:55,088
Pause and solve.

110
00:05:00,488 --> 00:05:01,961
The answer is B.

111
00:05:01,961 --> 00:05:03,194
Five by four.

112
00:05:03,694 --> 00:05:07,270
The perimeter of a sector
is two radii plus the arc.

113
00:05:07,270 --> 00:05:09,745
So two r plus l equals twenty six.

114
00:05:10,245 --> 00:05:14,066
The arc is two more than the
radius, so l is r plus two.

115
00:05:14,066 --> 00:05:17,580
Then three r plus two is
twenty six, and r is eight.

116
00:05:18,080 --> 00:05:19,270
So l is ten.

117
00:05:19,270 --> 00:05:24,228
Theta is l over r, ten over eight,
which is five by four radians.

118
00:05:24,828 --> 00:05:26,288
Here is your recap.

119
00:05:26,288 --> 00:05:27,790
Screenshot this card.

120
00:05:30,990 --> 00:05:33,988
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

121
00:05:34,388 --> 00:05:35,288
One.

122
00:05:34,890 --> 00:05:37,885
How many degrees are
there in two full turns?

123
00:05:38,785 --> 00:05:39,685
Two.

124
00:05:39,320 --> 00:05:43,590
An arc of fifteen centimetres
sits on a circle of radius five.

125
00:05:43,590 --> 00:05:45,751
What is the angle in radians?

126
00:05:46,651 --> 00:05:47,551
Three.

127
00:05:47,304 --> 00:05:50,783
Why is one radian the
same size in every circle?

128
00:05:51,883 --> 00:05:56,387
Next time: half a wheel turn is one
hundred and eighty degrees, and also

129
00:05:56,387 --> 00:05:57,287
pi radians.

130
00:05:57,270 --> 00:05:59,700
So how do we switch between the two?
